By giving the constructors of the collision strategies the game’s manager, we are
giving the strategies access to all the public methods of the game manager. This
breaks encapsulation, by giving the strategies access to more functionality than it
actually needs. Also, this way the collision strategy is bound to the specific game (a
BrickerGameManager class), and can’t be reused in other projects.

We wrote a class called “Lives” to manage the lives of the player, counting the lives
in a single game. This separates the actual counting (increment/decrement) of the
lives, from its representation. The lives are incremented/decremented through a strict
API (incrementHearts, decrementHearts), which makes sure that the two representations
are updated correctly alongside the actual counter.

======================================================================================
A. Roles of Added Classes and Interfaces
======================================================================================

Package brick_strategies:
-------------------------
* ExplosiveCollisionStrategy: Strategy that removes the brick and triggers a chain
  reaction explosion on neighboring bricks.
* ExtraLifeCollisionStrategy: Strategy that removes the brick and creates a falling
  heart object (FallingHeart).
* ExtraPaddleStrategy: Strategy that removes the brick and triggers the creation of
  an extra paddle at the center of the screen.
* PuckStrategy: Strategy that removes the brick and triggers the creation of two Puck
  balls at the center of the latest removed brick.

Package factories:
------------------
* BrickStrategyFactory: Factory class that randomly assigns a collision strategy to
  each brick according to the specified probability distribution.
* BallFactory: Factory for instantiating the main game ball (Ball) and puck balls
  (Puck) with their required properties (size, texture, center position, and initial
  velocity).
* PaddleFactory: Factory for instantiating the user-controlled main paddle
  (UserPaddle) and extra paddle (ExtraPaddle).

Package gameobjects:
--------------------
* Paddle (Abstract Class): Base class for paddles enforcing screen boundary
  restrictions (minX, maxX).
* UserPaddle: Extends Paddle. Main paddle controlled by the player using the left and
  right arrow keys.
* ExtraPaddle: Secondary paddle created at the center of the screen that automatically
  removes itself after 4 ball collisions.
* Puck: Secondary ball (scaled to 75% size of the main ball) that removes itself when
  falling past the bottom screen boundary.
* Brick: Represents a single brick in the game, triggers its strategy upon collision,
  and tracks its row/column position.
* BrickGrid: Manages the 2D matrix of bricks, tracks active brick count, and handles
  explosive chain reactions.
* FallingHeart: Heart object that falls vertically, collides strictly with the main
  paddle, and restores a life.
* Lives: Encapsulates and displays the player's life count (both numeric text and
  graphical heart icons).
* Wall: A wall object to enable the ball and paddle to collide with it.
* Border: Constructs the side walls.
* Background: Background layer.

======================================================================================
B. Player Lives Implementation and Future Extensions
======================================================================================

Implementation:
---------------
The Lives class centrally encapsulates the player's life state:
* Graphical Display: Maintains a list of heart GameObjects added to/removed from
  Layer.BACKGROUND.
* Numeric Display: Uses a TextRenderable object displaying the numerical count and
  dynamically updating its color (green for 3+, yellow for 2, red for 1).
* Logic: Provides incrementHearts() and decrementHearts() methods to update the life
  counter (capped at a maximum of 4 lives) and UI representations simultaneously.

Extensibility for Future Life-Dependent Components:
---------------------------------------------------
The Lives class exposes a clean public API (isGameOver(), incrementHearts(),
decrementHearts()). Game logic coordination is managed through BrickerGameManager. Any
future component dependent on lives (e.g., audio triggers, extra UI widgets, or loss
indicators) can query or interact with this API without modifying the internal
graphical representation or internal logic of the Lives class (adhering to the
Open-Closed Principle).

======================================================================================
Implementation of Special Behaviors and Associated Classes
======================================================================================

Extra Balls (Puck Balls):
-------------------------
* Implementation: On colliding with a brick configured with PuckStrategy,
  createPucks() is called on BrickerGameManager. The manager invokes
  BallFactory.createPuck() to instantiate two Puck balls at the center position of the
  broken brick.
* Classes Added:
  - PuckStrategy: Strategy class triggering puck creation.
  - Puck: Subclass of Ball. Overrides update() to check if the puck's Y position
    exceeds the window height (WINDOW_HEIGHT). If out of bounds, it removes itself
    from gameObjects.
  - BallFactory: Initializes pucks at 75% size of the main ball with random initial
    velocities directed in the upper semicircle.

Extra Paddle:
-------------
* Implementation: Upon colliding with a brick configured with ExtraPaddleStrategy,
  createExtraPaddle() is called on BrickerGameManager. The manager checks if an extra
  paddle is already active (if (this.extraPaddle != null) return;). If not, a new
  extra paddle is created at the center of the screen.
* Classes Added:
  - ExtraPaddleStrategy: Strategy class triggering extra paddle creation.
  - ExtraPaddle: Subclass of UserPaddle. Maintains a collision counter
    (numCollisions). Inside onCollisionEnter(), it increments the counter whenever
    colliding with a Ball (including Puck). Upon reaching 4 collisions, it removes
    itself from the game (gameObjects.removeGameObject(this)).
  - PaddleFactory: Instantiates the extra paddle centered in the game window.

Explosive Brick:
----------------
* Implementation: Upon colliding with a brick configured with
  ExplosiveCollisionStrategy, removeBrick() is called on BrickerGameManager with
  isExplosive = true.
* Classes Added/Modified:
  - ExplosiveCollisionStrategy: Strategy defining explosive brick behavior.
  - BrickGrid: Manages the 2D brick array. When an explosive brick is removed, it plays
    an explosion sound effect (explosion.wav) and triggers pseudoCollision() on
    neighbor bricks in the grid: [i-1, j], [i+1, j], [i, j-1], and [i, j+1].
  - Brick: Added pseudoCollision() method to trigger neighbor strategies without
    requiring a physical ball collision (enabling chain explosions).

Extra Life (Falling Heart):
---------------------------
* Implementation: Upon breaking a brick configured with ExtraLifeCollisionStrategy,
  startFallingHeart() is called on BrickerGameManager. A heart object is created at the
  destroyed brick's center position and falls downward.
* Classes Added:
  - ExtraLifeCollisionStrategy: Strategy class creating the falling heart.
  - FallingHeart: Subclass of GameObject.
    * Movement: Moves downward at a constant vertical speed of 100.
    * Collision Filtering: Overrides shouldCollideWith(GameObject other) to return true
      only if the colliding object is the main paddle (userPaddle).
    * Collision Handling: On collision with the main paddle, calls
      manager.incrementHearts() (restoring a life up to max 4) and removes itself. If
      it falls past the bottom screen boundary without colliding, it removes itself
      automatically.

======================================================================================
API Extensions
======================================================================================

1. Extensions to the Brick API:
-------------------------------
* Added getCenterCoordinates(): Some behaviors (such as creating Puck balls or
  FallingHeart objects) require instantiating new game objects at the exact center
  location of the destroyed brick.
* Added pseudoCollision(): Necessary for the Explosive Brick behavior. When an
  explosive brick goes KABOOM, it needs to trigger the destruction of neighboring
  bricks in the BrickGrid without requiring a physical ball collision event or mocking
  fake Collision objects. pseudoCollision() allows neighboring bricks to execute their
  assigned collision strategies cleanly.
* Added getRow() and getCol(): Explicit accessors for row and column indices required
  by BrickGrid to locate adjacent neighbors during explosive chain reactions.

2. Extensions to the BrickerGameManager API:
-------------------------------------------
* Added Object Creators & Game State Methods (createPucks, createExtraPaddle,
  startFallingHeart, incrementHearts): Strategies (CollisionStrategy) execute during
  brick destruction. Extending BrickerGameManager provided a clean bridge for
  strategies to request game-level object additions and state modifications.
* Added Layer Management Helpers (addBackgroundObj, removeBackgroundObj, addStaticObj,
  removeStaticObj): Required for objects like Lives (heart icons in the background
  layer) and FallingHeart / ExtraPaddle to manage their lifecycle and layer placement
  through the manager cleanly.